    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0007",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 7,
    "stem": "A 62-year old diabetic was admitted because of fever with right flank pains and chills. She also experienced dysuria and loss of appetite. BP: 120/70PR: 22/min T: 38.5 no crackles, No S4, (+) right CVA tenderness. CBC: Hgb: 140 Hct: 0.40 WBC 20000/mL with neutrophilia. Urinalysis: Pus cells 40-50/hpf; bacteria: 4+. Urine gram stain showed gram negative rods. Serum creatinine: 0.8 mg/dL. In the ward, IV ciprofloxacin was administered. Three days later, she still was febrile and has persistent R flank pain. Urine C & S result was still unavailable. Which ancillary procedure is BEST to do this time?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "CT scan with contrast of the abdomen"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Repeat urine C & S"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Repeat serum creatinine"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Ultrasound of KUB"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "An ultrasound of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder (KUB) is a non-invasive imaging modality that does not involve contrast. It can provide information about the renal structures, detect obstruction, and assess the urinary tract. Given the patient's persistent symptoms and the concern for obstruction, an ultrasound can be a valuable diagnostic tool without the risk of contrast-induced nephropathy.\nIf after treatment and management, there is no improvement in 72 hours, imaging (ultrasound) is recommended to check for obstruction.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "In a patient with renal injury, the use of contrast in imaging studies may pose a risk of contrast-induced nephropathy. Given the patient's history of fever, right flank pains, and chills with persistent symptoms, a CT scan with contrast could provide detailed information about the genitourinary system, including the kidneys and urinary tract. However, caution is warranted due to the risk of contrast-induced nephropathy.",
      "B": "A repeat urine culture and sensitivity (C & S) test is crucial to reevaluate the presence of infection and determine the antibiotic sensitivity of the causative organism. This can guide the choice of antibiotics for optimal treatment. It is essential to ensure that the selected antibiotic is effective against the identified bacteria. However, it takes time before results arrive and it is not the best management in this case.",
      "C": "Monitoring serum creatinine levels is important in assessing renal function, especially in the context of the patient's symptoms and potential renal involvement. However, while serum creatinine provides information about renal function, it may not directly assess the presence or cause of persistent symptoms.",
      "D": "An ultrasound of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder (KUB) is a non-invasive imaging modality that does not involve contrast. It can provide information about the renal structures, detect obstruction, and assess the urinary tract. Given the patient's persistent symptoms and the concern for obstruction, an ultrasound can be a valuable diagnostic tool without the risk of contrast-induced nephropathy.\nIf after treatment and management, there is no improvement in 72 hours, imaging (ultrasound) is recommended to check for obstruction."
    },
