    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Unilateral ureterolithiasis, especially in a patient with a solitary kidney, can lead to azotemia. In a solitary kidney, any obstruction affecting urinary flow from that kidney can result in elevated levels of blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and serum creatinine.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Non-obstructing nephrolithiasis on both kidneys is less likely to result in azotemia. Azotemia is often associated with obstructive uropathy or conditions that significantly compromise the renal function.",
      "B": "Unilateral renal staghorn calculus, if causing obstruction, may lead to azotemia. Staghorn calculi are large, branching stones that occupy a large portion of the renal pelvis and the branches of the collecting system. If these stones obstruct the urinary flow, it can result in impaired renal function.",
      "C": "Urolithiasis at the distal right ureter may cause obstruction, potentially leading to azotemia. The site of the obstruction and its impact on urinary flow are crucial determinants.",
      "D": "Unilateral ureterolithiasis, especially in a patient with a solitary kidney, can lead to azotemia. In a solitary kidney, any obstruction affecting urinary flow from that kidney can result in elevated levels of blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and serum creatinine."
    },
