    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0042",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 42,
    "stem": "If the result tells you there is a mass lesion present on the cervical spine area, which test do you request for?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Excision of the mass with biopsy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "CSF analysis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "CT guided biopsy of the mass"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "PET scan"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "CT-guided biopsy is a procedure where a biopsy needle is guided by computed tomography to obtain tissue samples from a specific area. This is a minimally invasive approach and is particularly useful when the mass is deep-seated or not easily accessible. It allows for a targeted sampling of the mass.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Excision of a mass with biopsy is a definitive procedure to obtain tissue for histopathological examination. However, the decision to proceed with surgical excision is usually based on a comprehensive evaluation, including imaging studies and clinical considerations. This may be considered after identifying the nature and extent of the mass.",
      "B": "CSF (Cerebrospinal Fluid) analysis involves obtaining a sample of the fluid surrounding the brain and spinal cord. It is useful in evaluating certain neurological conditions, such as infections or inflammatory processes. However, if there is a mass lesion in the cervical spine area, CSF analysis may not directly provide information about the nature or location of the mass.",
      "C": "CT-guided biopsy is a procedure where a biopsy needle is guided by computed tomography to obtain tissue samples from a specific area. This is a minimally invasive approach and is particularly useful when the mass is deep-seated or not easily accessible. It allows for a targeted sampling of the mass.",
      "D": "PET (Positron Emission Tomography) scan is a nuclear medicine imaging technique that provides functional information about tissues. It is often used in cancer evaluation to identify areas with increased metabolic activity. A PET scan may be useful in determining the metabolic activity of the mass, aiding in the assessment of its nature."
    },
