    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0047",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 47,
    "stem": "Situation 3 - Teddy Ver, 33 years old, BMI of 30, consults you due to epigastric pain radiating to the back following a meal of lechon and kare-kare. He has been experiencing abdominal fullness and discomfort with burping more often lately. PE: normal VS, anicteric sclerae, (+) Murphy’s sign, CBC showed leukocytosis with neutrophilia. Urinalysis was normal. Which diagnosis do you consider Teddy Ver has?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Calculous cholecystitis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Peptic ulcer disease"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Acute appendicitis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Gastroesophageal reflux"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Given Teddy Ver's symptoms of epigastric pain radiating to the back, abdominal fullness, and discomfort, along with a meal history that includes fatty foods (lechon and kare-kare), calculous cholecystitis is a likely consideration. The positive Murphy's sign, indicative of pain upon palpation of the gallbladder, aligns with this diagnosis. Leukocytosis with neutrophilia in the CBC supports an inflammatory process.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Given Teddy Ver's symptoms of epigastric pain radiating to the back, abdominal fullness, and discomfort, along with a meal history that includes fatty foods (lechon and kare-kare), calculous cholecystitis is a likely consideration. The positive Murphy's sign, indicative of pain upon palpation of the gallbladder, aligns with this diagnosis. Leukocytosis with neutrophilia in the CBC supports an inflammatory process.",
      "B": "Peptic ulcer disease involves the development of open sores (ulcers) in the stomach or the first part of the small intestine. While it can cause epigastric pain, the described symptoms of radiating pain to the back and abdominal fullness may not be typical for peptic ulcer disease. The association with a fatty meal could also point toward gallbladder involvement rather than peptic ulcer.",
      "C": "Acute appendicitis is less likely in this case, as the symptoms are more suggestive of upper gastrointestinal involvement (epigastric pain, abdominal fullness). The absence of symptoms typically associated with appendicitis, such as right lower quadrant pain and nausea, makes this diagnosis less probable.",
      "D": "Gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) involves the backward flow of stomach contents into the esophagus, causing symptoms like heartburn and regurgitation. While GERD can cause epigastric discomfort and may be exacerbated by certain foods, the described symptoms of radiating pain to the back and a positive Murphy's sign are not typical for GERD."
    },
