    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0048",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 48,
    "stem": "To make a definitive diagnosis which procedure will you do first?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Ultrasound of liver, gallbladder, pancreas"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Serum amylase and lipase"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "CT scan of the abdomen"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Gastroscopy"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "An ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging modality that can provide valuable information about the structure and function of the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. It is commonly used to assess for gallstones, inflammation, or other abnormalities. In the context of Teddy Ver's symptoms, an ultrasound is a reasonable first step to visualize the gallbladder and assess for signs of calculous cholecystitis.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "An ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging modality that can provide valuable information about the structure and function of the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. It is commonly used to assess for gallstones, inflammation, or other abnormalities. In the context of Teddy Ver's symptoms, an ultrasound is a reasonable first step to visualize the gallbladder and assess for signs of calculous cholecystitis.",
      "B": "Serum amylase and lipase levels are blood tests that can aid in the diagnosis of pancreatitis. While pancreatitis could be a consideration, the described symptoms and findings, such as a positive Murphy's sign, are more suggestive of gallbladder involvement. Serum amylase and lipase may be ordered as part of a comprehensive workup, but they may not provide a definitive diagnosis for calculous cholecystitis.",
      "C": "A CT scan is a more detailed imaging modality that can provide cross-sectional images of the abdominal organs. It is particularly useful for assessing the extent of inflammation and detecting complications. While a CT scan can be valuable, it is generally not the first-line imaging study for suspected calculous cholecystitis unless there are specific indications or if ultrasound results are inconclusive.",
      "D": "Gastroscopy, or esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD), is a procedure involving the insertion of a flexible tube with a camera into the upper digestive tract. This procedure is more focused on assessing the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum. While it can identify conditions such as peptic ulcers or inflammation, it may not provide a direct view of the gallbladder and is not the primary diagnostic tool for calculous cholecystitis."
    },
