    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0049",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 49,
    "stem": "While waiting to be admitted which treatment will you initiate at the ER?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Anticholinergic"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Proton pump inhibitor"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Oral antibiotic"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Parenteral antibiotic"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "The initial treatment for calculous cholecystitis often includes parenteral (intravenous) antibiotics to address the underlying infection promptly. This approach helps achieve higher and more immediate antibiotic concentrations in the bloodstream and tissues. Commonly used antibiotics include third-generation cephalosporins or a combination of ampicillin and sulbactam.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Anticholinergic medications may help alleviate symptoms related to smooth muscle spasms, but they are not the first-line treatment for calculous cholecystitis. In the acute setting, the focus is often on pain management and addressing the underlying infection. Anticholinergics are not the primary agents for these purposes.",
      "B": "Proton pump inhibitors reduce the production of stomach acid and are commonly used to treat conditions such as gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and peptic ulcers. While PPIs can be beneficial in managing symptoms associated with acid reflux, they are not the primary treatment for calculous cholecystitis. Gallbladder inflammation is best addressed with interventions targeting the infection.",
      "C": "While antibiotics are a crucial component of the treatment for calculous cholecystitis, the initial management in the emergency room often involves parenteral (intravenous) administration. Oral antibiotics may be considered for less severe cases or after the acute phase is managed. In the acute setting, the focus is on swift intervention, and intravenous antibiotics are generally preferred.",
      "D": "The initial treatment for calculous cholecystitis often includes parenteral (intravenous) antibiotics to address the underlying infection promptly. This approach helps achieve higher and more immediate antibiotic concentrations in the bloodstream and tissues. Commonly used antibiotics include third-generation cephalosporins or a combination of ampicillin and sulbactam."
    },
