    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0050",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 50,
    "stem": "Situation 4 - a 25 year old mason was rushed to the emergency room due to crush injury from a fallen cemented wall. There were hematomas on his trunk, buttocks, and extremities. Serum creatinine was 9. (0.6-1.1) mg/dL. If the fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) is more than 1% the diagnosis is most likely",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Post renal acute kidney injury"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Intrinsic acute kidney injury"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Pre-renal acute kidney injury"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Chronic kidney injury"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Intrinsic AKI results from damage to the kidney tissue itself. Crush injuries can lead to rhabdomyolysis, a condition where muscle breakdown products (myoglobin) are released into the bloodstream and can cause kidney damage. The elevated serum creatinine and the clinical scenario make intrinsic AKI, such as rhabdomyolysis-induced renal injury, a likely consideration.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Post-renal acute kidney injury (AKI) refers to a condition where there is obstruction of urine flow after the kidney, leading to impaired excretion. In the context of crush injury, hematomas on the trunk, buttocks, and extremities, post-renal causes such as urinary tract obstruction due to trauma or blood clots should be considered. However, the given information does not provide direct evidence of post-renal causes.",
      "B": "Intrinsic AKI results from damage to the kidney tissue itself. Crush injuries can lead to rhabdomyolysis, a condition where muscle breakdown products (myoglobin) are released into the bloodstream and can cause kidney damage. The elevated serum creatinine and the clinical scenario make intrinsic AKI, such as rhabdomyolysis-induced renal injury, a likely consideration.",
      "C": "Pre-renal AKI is often related to reduced blood flow to the kidneys, leading to decreased filtration. Hematomas from crush injuries can cause hypovolemia and decreased perfusion to the kidneys. However, the very high serum creatinine level of 9 mg/dL suggests severe kidney damage beyond what is typically seen in pre-renal causes alone.",
      "D": "Chronic kidney injury implies long-term damage to the kidneys, usually over a period of months or years. The clinical scenario described with a crush injury is more consistent with an acute event rather than a chronic process."
    },
