    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0051",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 51,
    "stem": "An ECG done showed tall peaked T waves. Which of the following do you need to determine?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Calcium"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Sodium"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Phosphorus"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Potassium"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Potassium is the electrolyte most closely associated with changes in the T wave on an ECG. Hyperkalemia (elevated potassium levels) can result in tall, peaked T waves. It's crucial to assess potassium levels, as hyperkalemia can have significant implications for cardiac function, potentially leading to arrhythmias and other cardiac complications.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Tall peaked T waves on an ECG can be associated with hyperkalemia, a condition characterized by elevated levels of potassium. While calcium plays a role in cardiac muscle function, it is not the primary electrolyte to investigate when tall peaked T waves are observed. Hyperkalemia affects the resting membrane potential of cardiac cells, leading to changes in the ECG, such as tall T waves.",
      "B": "Sodium primarily influences water balance and plays a crucial role in maintaining cell membrane potential. However, sodium imbalances are not typically associated with tall T waves on an ECG. Sodium abnormalities may present with different ECG findings or clinical symptoms.",
      "C": "Phosphorus imbalances are generally not directly associated with changes in the ECG, especially tall T waves. Phosphorus is essential for various cellular processes, including energy metabolism and bone health, but its influence on cardiac electrophysiology is not a common consideration in the context of ECG changes.",
      "D": "Potassium is the electrolyte most closely associated with changes in the T wave on an ECG. Hyperkalemia (elevated potassium levels) can result in tall, peaked T waves. It's crucial to assess potassium levels, as hyperkalemia can have significant implications for cardiac function, potentially leading to arrhythmias and other cardiac complications."
    },
