    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0053",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 53,
    "stem": "Situation 5 - Mr. Dee Lim, 60 chief executive officer consults you of nuchal pain and generalized headache. Blood pressure for 3 consecutive days was 170/100. On physical exam of the cardiovascular system, these are your findings: dynamic precordium, apex beat at 6th LICS 1cm from MCL, gr ⅙ systolic blow murmur at aortic area. These are suggestive of:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Left ventricular hypertrophy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Right ventricular hypertrophy"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Right arterial enlargement"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Left arterial enlargement"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "LVH is a marker of the pathophysiological response of the myocardium to the stimulus of chronic pressure overload, chronic volume overload, or both. As such, there are no signs or symptoms that result from LVH itself, but rather signs and symptoms related to the underlying pathological cause of LVH (e.g., elevated blood pressure, abnormal heart sounds including gallops and murmurs, etc.).",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "LVH is a marker of the pathophysiological response of the myocardium to the stimulus of chronic pressure overload, chronic volume overload, or both. As such, there are no signs or symptoms that result from LVH itself, but rather signs and symptoms related to the underlying pathological cause of LVH (e.g., elevated blood pressure, abnormal heart sounds including gallops and murmurs, etc.).",
      "B": "The findings mentioned, particularly the apex beat location and systolic murmur at the aortic area, are more suggestive of left ventricular involvement rather than right ventricular hypertrophy. Right ventricular hypertrophy is often associated with conditions such as chronic lung disease or pulmonary hypertension and presents with different clinical features.",
      "C": "Right atrial enlargement is not typically assessed through the described findings on the cardiovascular exam. It is often evaluated through electrocardiography (ECG) or imaging studies. The given information does not specifically point to right atrial enlargement.",
      "D": "Left atrial enlargement may be associated with left ventricular hypertrophy, but it is not typically assessed through physical exam findings like the apex beat location or systolic murmur. Left atrial enlargement is often evaluated through imaging studies or ECG."
    },
