    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0057",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 57,
    "stem": "To support your impression of osteoporosis, which test(s) will you order to be done?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Total bone scan"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Serum Vitamin D"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Serum calcium"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Bone densitometry"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Bone densitometry, often performed using Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA) scans, is the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis. It measures bone mineral density (BMD) at specific sites, usually the hip and spine, and provides information about the strength and density of bones. The results are used to assess fracture risk and guide treatment decisions.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "A total bone scan, also known as a whole-body bone scan, is a nuclear medicine imaging test that is often used to detect abnormalities in bone metabolism. While it can identify areas of increased or decreased bone activity, it is not the primary test for diagnosing osteoporosis. Total bone scans are more commonly used to detect conditions such as metastatic bone disease or infections.",
      "B": "Vitamin D plays a crucial role in bone health, and its deficiency can contribute to bone disorders, including osteoporosis. Measuring serum vitamin D levels can provide information about the patient's vitamin D status. However, a vitamin D test alone is not sufficient for diagnosing osteoporosis, and other tests such as bone densitometry are typically needed for a comprehensive evaluation of bone health.",
      "C": "Serum calcium levels are important for bone health, and abnormalities can be associated with various bone disorders. However, measuring serum calcium alone is not specific for osteoporosis. Elevated or low serum calcium levels can be seen in different conditions, and osteoporosis is typically diagnosed through assessments of bone density rather than serum calcium levels.",
      "D": "Bone densitometry, often performed using Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA) scans, is the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis. It measures bone mineral density (BMD) at specific sites, usually the hip and spine, and provides information about the strength and density of bones. The results are used to assess fracture risk and guide treatment decisions."
    },
