    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0076",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 76,
    "stem": "On admission, he changed to wear a hospital gown. You observed that he had varicose veins on his lower extremities. Which possible disease entity will you now consider as another possible cause of his present problem?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Pulmonary emboli, recurrent"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Bronchiectasis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Sleep apnea"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Neuromuscular disorder"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Recurrent pulmonary emboli could lead to chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH), a condition where pulmonary arteries become obstructed due to chronic blood clots. This can result in right ventricular strain and cor pulmonale. However, the presence of varicose veins on the lower extremities is not typically associated with recurrent pulmonary emboli.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Recurrent pulmonary emboli could lead to chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH), a condition where pulmonary arteries become obstructed due to chronic blood clots. This can result in right ventricular strain and cor pulmonale. However, the presence of varicose veins on the lower extremities is not typically associated with recurrent pulmonary emboli.",
      "B": "Bronchiectasis is a condition characterized by irreversible dilation of the bronchi, leading to chronic cough, sputum production, and recurrent respiratory infections. While bronchiectasis can contribute to chronic respiratory symptoms, it is not directly associated with varicose veins.",
      "C": "Sleep apnea is a sleep disorder characterized by intermittent cessation of breathing during sleep. It can lead to hypoxia and increased pulmonary artery pressure, potentially contributing to pulmonary hypertension. However, the presence of varicose veins on the lower extremities is not a typical feature of sleep apnea.",
      "D": "Certain neuromuscular disorders, such as muscular dystrophy or myasthenia gravis, can affect respiratory muscles, leading to respiratory insufficiency. However, varicose veins on the lower extremities are not directly associated with neuromuscular disorders."
    },
