    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0092",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 92,
    "stem": "Situation 18 - Jonathan had a Friday night out with his drinking buddies. When he arrived home he experienced severe epigastric pain radiating to the back accompanied by nausea, vomiting and abdominal distention. While at the Emergency Room which blood exam will you request for to rule in your diagnosis of acute pancreatitis:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Complete blood count"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Serum amylase/lipase"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Serum creatinine"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Serum electrolytes"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Serum Amylase and Lipase are key markers for diagnosing acute pancreatitis. Elevated levels of these enzymes in the blood suggest pancreatic inflammation. Lipase, in particular, is considered more specific to the pancreas. The diagnosis is often confirmed if amylase and lipase levels are significantly elevated.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "A complete blood count may show elevated white blood cell count, indicating inflammation or infection, but it is not specific to pancreatitis. It is often part of the initial workup to assess overall health.",
      "B": "Serum Amylase and Lipase are key markers for diagnosing acute pancreatitis. Elevated levels of these enzymes in the blood suggest pancreatic inflammation. Lipase, in particular, is considered more specific to the pancreas. The diagnosis is often confirmed if amylase and lipase levels are significantly elevated.",
      "C": "Serum creatinine is a marker of kidney function. While kidney function is important in the overall assessment of a patient, it is not a specific marker for pancreatitis. However, acute pancreatitis can lead to kidney complications, so monitoring creatinine levels is relevant in the broader context.",
      "D": "Serum electrolytes, including sodium and potassium, are important for assessing overall metabolic status. Severe cases of pancreatitis can lead to electrolyte imbalances, so monitoring these levels is important, but it is not specific to pancreatitis."
    },
