    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0095",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 95,
    "stem": "Situation 19: Donald Dock, a tourist went surfing in Siargao for one week. On his 8th day, he experienced chills, fever, cough and dyspnea. BP:120/80 RR:28/min T:39.5 PR:90 Flaring of alae nasi, right basal crackles, regular rhythm, no cyanosis. With the above symptoms and physical exam findings, what is your most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Acute viral illness"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Pneumonia"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Tuberculosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Infectious mononucleosis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Pneumonia is a likely consideration given the symptoms of chills, fever, cough, dyspnea, and the presence of right basal crackles. Pneumonia is characterized by inflammation of the lung parenchyma and can be caused by various pathogens, including bacteria, viruses, and fungi.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Acute Viral Illness can present with symptoms such as chills, fever, cough, and dyspnea. However, the presence of right basal crackles on physical examination may suggest a lower respiratory tract involvement, which is less typical for many viral infections.",
      "B": "Pneumonia is a likely consideration given the symptoms of chills, fever, cough, dyspnea, and the presence of right basal crackles. Pneumonia is characterized by inflammation of the lung parenchyma and can be caused by various pathogens, including bacteria, viruses, and fungi.",
      "C": "Tuberculosis (TB) can present with symptoms similar to those mentioned, including fever and cough. However, TB is often associated with more chronic symptoms, and crackles on physical examination may not be as commonly observed in the acute phase.",
      "D": "Infectious Mononucleosis is caused by the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) and can present with symptoms such as fever, sore throat, and fatigue. However, dyspnea and crackles on lung examination are not typical features of infectious mononucleosis."
    },
