    "id": "notion-internal-medicine-0100",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 100,
    "stem": "She asks your advice since her sister in law who stays with her is pregnant. What do you advise her?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "She can tell her to leave for one month before resuming office work"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "She will need post exposure prophylaxis with varicella immune globulin"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Preemptive administration of acyclovir"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "She will need varicella vaccine only"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Varicella immune globulin (VZIG) is a passive immunization option that can provide temporary protection against chickenpox if administered within a certain timeframe after exposure. However, its use is generally reserved for specific high-risk groups, and it may not be routinely recommended in healthy pregnant women.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "While it is important to prevent the transmission of chickenpox to susceptible individuals, advising someone to leave for a month might not be practical. Chickenpox is highly contagious, and transmission occurs before the onset of the rash. It's challenging to predict the exact timing of when someone with chickenpox becomes non-infectious.",
      "B": "Varicella immune globulin (VZIG) is a passive immunization option that can provide temporary protection against chickenpox if administered within a certain timeframe after exposure. However, its use is generally reserved for specific high-risk groups, and it may not be routinely recommended in healthy pregnant women.",
      "C": "Preemptive administration of acyclovir is not a typical approach for post-exposure prophylaxis in chickenpox. Acyclovir is more commonly used as a treatment for active infections.",
      "D": "The varicella vaccine is a preventive measure, but it is not typically administered post-exposure. It is part of routine immunization schedules to protect against chickenpox."
    },
