    "id": "superexam-internal-medicine-0030",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 30,
    "stem": "A sexually active 24-year-old woman known to be\nHIV-1 infected has had a fever for 2 days and has a\nproductive cough. Chest radiographs show an\ninfiltrate in the right lung. Two weeks earlier, her\nhelper T cell count was 510/mm3. Testing for\nnontreponemal antigen (rapid plasma reagin) is\npositive at two dilutions, and treponemal antigen\ntesting is also positive. Which of the following is the\nmost likely cause of the pneumonia?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Pneumocystis carinii"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Syphilis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The most likely cause of the pneumonia is\nStreptococcus pneumoniae. Although P. carinii is\nthe most common serious opportunistic infection\nin patients with HIV-1 infection, the productive\ncough, localized infi ltrate, and brief duration\nargue against P. carinii pneumonia. In addition, the\nCD4 count of more than 500 cells/mm3 suggests\nthat serious\nopportunistic infections such as PCP,\ncytomegalovirus, or Mycobacterium avium-\nintracellulare are unlikely for some time. Syphilis,\nwhich may have been present, almost never\ninvolves the lungs.",
