    "id": "superexam-internal-medicine-0031",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 31,
    "stem": "A 75-year-old man complains of chest pain while\nclimbing stairs. On physical examination, there is a\nII/VI systolic ejection murmur that radiates to the\nneck. The carotid upstrokes are delayed and\ndiminished in volume. The likely diagnosis is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Aortic stenosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Mitral stenosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Pulmonary stenosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Pulmonic regurgitation"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The murmur and delayed carotid upstrokes are\ntypical of the fixed LV outflow obstruction of aortic\nstenosis. Pulmonary stenosis also can cause chest\npain and a systolic ejection murmur but would not\ncause carotid delay. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\ncauses a spike and dome of the carotid upstrokes,\nthat is, a sharp upstroke followed by fall and a\nflatter secondary rise. The murmur of mitral\nstenosis is diastolic.",
