    "id": "superexam-internal-medicine-0062",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 62,
    "stem": "All of the ff statements regarding principles of\ncancer diagnosis is true EXCEPT?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The diagnosis of cancer relies most heavily on\ninvasive tissue biopsy and should never be made\nwithout obtaining tissues"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "No noninvasive diagnostic test is sufficient to\ndefine a disease process as cancer"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "The first priority in patient management after the\ndiagnosis of cancer is established and shared with\nthe patient is to determine the extent of disease."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "The curability of a tumor usually is inversely\nproportional to the tumor burden"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "The most widely used system of staging for\ncancers such as Lung Cancer, Breast Cancer,\nHodgkin's disease and Colorectal cancer is the TNM\n(tumor, node, metastasis) system"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "E",
    "explanation": "As stated vebatim in Harrison's 19th ed, pg 468-\n470, The most widely used system of staging is the\nTNM (tumor, node, metastasis) system for most\ncancers. Other anatomic staging systems are used\nfor some tumors, e.g., the Dukes classification for\ncolorectal cancers, the International Federation of\nGynecologists and Obstetricians classification for\ngynecologic cancers, and the Ann Arbor\nclassification for Hodgkin’s disease.",
