    "id": "superexam-internal-medicine-0309",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 309,
    "stem": "Which of the following is not a potential\ncomplication of patient who underwent massive\nresection of small bowel due to superior mesenteric\nartery occlusion?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Calcium oxalate renal stones"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Cholesterol gallstones"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Progressive coronary atherosclerosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "gastric ulcers"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "none of the above"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "this patient may develop short bowel syndrome\nfollowing resection of larger portion of the small\nand large intestine. Multiple factors contribute to\ndiarrhea and steatorrhea including gastric acid\nhypersecretion, increased bile acids in the small\nbowel due to decreased absorption in the\nremaining bowel. and lactose intolerance due to\ngastric acid hypersecretion. the propensity to\ndevelop oxalate renal stones is because of the\nbinding of free fatty acids in the colon to the\ncalcium. thus the calcium is not free to bind to\noxalate and the latter is easily absorbed in the\nlarge intestine. The increase bile acid pool size will\nyield to supersaturation of gallbladder contents\nleading to cholesterol gallstones. loss of inhibitory\ntransmitters coming from the small intestine will\nmake the gastric acid secretion unchecked. there is\nno relation between atherosclerosis and\nshortbowel syndrome.",
