    "id": "superexam-internal-medicine-0549",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 549,
    "stem": "A 25/M, recently diagnosed case of type 1 DM,\ncomes in for routine follow up after he noted passing\nfoamy urine. You order a urinalysis which reveals 3+\nproteinuria. You refer the patient to an\nOphthalmologist who found no evidence of\nretinopathy. The most likely cause of proteinuria in\nthe patient is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Diabetic nephropathy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Hypertensive kidney disease"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Minimal change disease"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Membranous glomerulonephritis"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Usually, DM nephropathy develops after 10 years\nduration; occurring in the presence of other\nmicrovascular complications, e.g. retinopathy.\nPEARL: Presence of nephropathy in diabetic in the\nabsence of eye disease should warrant\ninvestigation for other causes of nephropathy. The\nmost common cause of nephrotic syndrome in\nadults is MGN; the most common cause in kids is\nMCD.",
