    "id": "superexam-internal-medicine-0685",
    "subject": "internal-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 685,
    "stem": "A 69 yo man with a history of coronary artery\ndisease and hypertension presents with acute onset\nof right facial weakness and numbness. On\nexamination, his speech and extremity strength are\nnormal, but he has significant weakness of the right\nside of the face, including the orbicularis oculi. In\naddition, he complains of roaring in the right ear,\nand his taste sensation is absent on the right side of\nthe anterior tongue. Sensation is normal to\nprinprick. Which of the following would best explain\nthese findings?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Lacunar stroke of the left internal capsule"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Brainstem glioma"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Bell’s palsy"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "A stroke due to occlusion of the left middle\ncerebral artery"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Answer: C. Bell’s Palsy\nNotes: Bell’s palsy is idiopathic and nearly always\nacute. All of the patient’s deficits are referable to\nthe peripheral nervous system, including the loss\nof taste (chorda tympani branch of the facial\nnerve) and hyperacusis (branch of the stapedius\nmuscle of the ear).",
