    "originalNumber": 697,
    "stem": "The ECG of a 66 yr-old male with a history of\natherosclerotic heart disease reveals an irregular\nbut rapid heart rate. The QRS complexes are normal\nbut no P waves can be seen. The most likely reason\nfor this finding is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Atrial fibrillation"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Right bundle branch block"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Sinus tachycardia"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Answer: A\nA-fib is a continuous chaotic re-entry of electrical\nimpulses within the atrial myocardium that arises\nin a diseased or stretched L atrium. The chaotic\npatterns of atrial excitation prevents P-waves\nfrom being seen in the ECG. He ventricular\nresponse is rapid and irregular. In all other\nchoices, p wave should be distinguishable.",
